body_markdown:
To send a custom Stripe payment-failed email, verify the


` invoice.payment_failed


` webhook, retrieve the invoice’s current state, and render a React email with Unlayer Elements before handing the HTML to your delivery provider. Keep the recovery link pointed at your authenticated billing page—not an expiring portal session. Stripe supplies the billing event; Elements supplies the content; your application owns eligibility and retries.


This tutorial starts with an email preview, then adds a sandbox-only webhook that sends through Resend. The narrow first version handles automatically collected USD invoices. It is not a production notification queue, and it deliberately refuses older events rather than replaying them outside its short-window duplicate protection.


*Stripe supplies the event; Next.js verifies it; Elements renders the message; Resend submits it.*


*The payment-failed email rendered with Unlayer Elements: a navy header, a clear amount due, and one coral Review billing button.*


## What do you need before writing the email?


Use an existing TypeScript Next.js application with the App Router, a Stripe sandbox, and a Resend API key and verified sending domain. Your application must already have a working


` /settings/billing


` page: it authenticates the visitor, checks which billing account they may manage, and offers a way to update payment details. The example does not implement your login or account-to-Stripe-customer mapping.


Install the packages in that application:


```text
npm     install   @unlayer/react-elements stripe resend
```


Keep the resulting lockfile. Elements supports React and React DOM 18 or later; use the modern JSX configuration documented in its


[installation guide](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/installation) . No visual-builder SDK is needed because developers own this layout in code. Customer drag-and-drop editing would be a separate commercial-builder integration.


## How do you build the payment-failed email in React?


Create


` emails/payment-failed.tsx


` . Keep the initial message small: explain the failed attempt, show a USD amount, and give the customer one useful action. Avoid declaring their account cancelled or predicting the next retry from a single failure event.


The layout below uses the navy-and-coral design shown above. Optional plan, payment-method and invoice labels let a preview show example details without hard-coding those details into real notifications. The webhook later in the tutorial supplies only the amount and billing URL, so those optional labels are omitted. Replace the Northstar branding with your own before sending.


```text
import     {
Email  ,     Row  ,     Column  ,     ColumnLayouts  ,     Heading  ,     Paragraph  ,
Button  ,     Divider  ,   renderToHtmlParts  ,
