```


Keep the direct


` Email → Row → Column


` structure. Unlayer’s


[rendering API](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/rendering-api) recommends


` renderToHtmlParts


` for sending email; its documented composition puts the returned head and body into the document. The plain-text companion here is explicitly authored from the same values and retains the destination URL.


The USD restriction matters.


[Stripe amounts use currency minor units](https://docs.stripe.com/currencies) ; dividing every currency by 100 would be wrong for zero-decimal currencies. Add an explicit currency-aware formatter before expanding this example beyond USD.


## How can you preview it without sending anything?


Create


` app/email-preview/route.ts


` :


```text
import     {   paymentFailedEmail   }     from     '../../emails/payment-failed'  ;


export     const   runtime   =     'nodejs'  ;


export     async     function     GET  (  )     {
const   message   =     paymentFailedEmail  (  {
amountCents  :     4900  ,
billingUrl  :     'https://app.example.com/settings/billing'  ,
planLabel  :     'Northstar Pro · Monthly subscription'  ,
paymentMethodLabel  :     'Visa ending in 4242'  ,
invoiceLabel  :     'NS-1042'  ,
}  )  ;
return     new     Response  (  message  .  html  ,     {
headers  :     {
'Content-Type'  :     'text/html; charset=utf-8'  ,
'Cache-Control'  :     'no-store'  ,
}  ,
}  )  ;
}
```


Open


` /email-preview


` through your application’s development server. The preview uses Northstar branding, a USD 49.00 amount, and one Review billing button. Use your own application origin and labels before sending. This route neither reads Stripe customer data nor calls Resend. Remove it from production if you do not want a public demonstration endpoint.


Use this first pass to settle copy and spacing. A browser preview is not an inbox-compatibility guarantee. Review the eventual email in the clients your product supports, especially after introducing images, fonts or longer content.


*The Northstar payment-failed design in the real Elements Playground, with JSX beside the email preview. The screenshot shows the styled demonstration layout; the application template above adds optional labels and a reminder to check the latest billing status.*


*The sandbox route handles the upper sequence. Durable intake, frozen payloads and reconciliation belong to the production application.*


## How do you connect the Stripe payment-failed webhook?


Configure these server-only environment variables through your local secret mechanism. Do not prefix them with


` NEXT_PUBLIC_


` or commit their values:


` STRIPE_SECRET_KEY


` for your sandbox,


` STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET


` ,


` RESEND_API_KEY


` ,


` MAIL_FROM


` on your verified domain, and


` APP_ORIGIN


` set to your real HTTPS origin.


Create


` app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts


` . This code rejects live events: keep that guard until you replace the demonstration’s synchronous processing with a durable workflow.


```text
import     Stripe     from     'stripe'  ;
import     {     Resend     }     from     'resend'  ;
import     {   paymentFailedEmail   }     from     '../../../../emails/payment-failed'  ;


export     const   runtime   =     'nodejs'  ;
const     required     =     (  name  :   string  )     =>     {
const   value   =   process  .  env  [  name  ]  ;
if     (  !  value  )     throw     new     Error  (  `  Set   ${  name  }  `  )  ;
return   value  ;
}  ;


export     async     function     POST  (  request  :     Request  )     {
const   signature   =   request  .  headers  .  get  (  'stripe-signature'  )  ;
if     (  !  signature  )     return     new     Response  (  'Missing signature'  ,     {     status  :     400     }  )  ;
const   stripe   =     new     Stripe  (  required  (  'STRIPE_SECRET_KEY'  )  )  ;
let     event  :     Stripe  .  Event  ;
try     {
event   =   stripe  .  webhooks  .  constructEvent  (
await   request  .  text  (  )  ,   signature  ,     required  (  'STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET'  )  ,
)  ;
}     catch     {
return     new     Response  (  'Invalid webhook'  ,     {     status  :     400     }  )  ;
}
if     (  event  .  livemode  )     return     new     Response  (  'Sandbox only'  ,     {     status  :     400     }  )  ;
if     (  event  .  type     !==     'invoice.payment_failed'  )     {
return     Response  .  json  (  {     ignored  :     true     }  )  ;
}
// A conservative demo cutoff, not a durable processing record.
if     (  Date  .  now  (  )     /     1000     -   event  .  created     >=     23     *     60     *     60  )     {
return     new     Response  (  'Needs manual reconciliation'  ,     {     status  :     500     }  )  ;
}
try     {
const   snapshot   =   event  .  data  .  object     as     Stripe  .  Invoice  ;
const   invoice   =     await   stripe  .  invoices  .  retrieve  (  snapshot  .  id  )  ;
if     (  invoice  .  status     !==     'open'     ||   invoice  .  amount_due     <=     0
||   invoice  .  collection_method     !==     'charge_automatically'  )     {
return     Response  .  json  (  {     skipped  :     true     }  )  ;
}
if     (  invoice  .  currency     !==     'usd'  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Unsupported currency'  )  ;
const   customerId   =     typeof   invoice  .  customer     ===     'string'
?   invoice  .  customer     :   invoice  .  customer  ?.  id  ;
if     (  !  customerId  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Missing customer'  )  ;
const   customer   =     await   stripe  .  customers  .  retrieve  (  customerId  )  ;
if     (  customer  .  deleted     ||     !  customer  .  email  )     throw     new     Error  (  'Missing recipient'  )  ;


const   origin   =     new     URL  (  required  (  'APP_ORIGIN'  )  )  ;
if     (  origin  .  protocol     !==     'https:'     ||   origin  .  username     ||   origin  .  password
||   origin  .  pathname     !==     '/'     ||   origin  .  search     ||   origin  .  hash  )     {
throw     new     Error  (  'APP_ORIGIN must be an HTTPS origin'  )  ;
}
const   message   =     paymentFailedEmail  (  {
amountCents  :   invoice  .  amount_due  ,
billingUrl  :     new     URL  (  '/settings/billing'  ,   origin  )  .  href  ,
}  )  ;
const   resend   =     new     Resend  (  required  (  'RESEND_API_KEY'  )  )  ;
const     {   data  ,   error   }     =     await   resend  .  emails  .  send  (  {
from  :     required  (  'MAIL_FROM'  )  ,     to  :     [  customer  .  email  ]  ,     ...  message  ,
}  ,     {     idempotencyKey  :     `  stripe-payment-failed/  ${  event  .  id  }  `     }  )  ;
if     (  error   ||     !  data  ?.  id  )     throw     new     Error  (  'No provider acknowledgement'  )  ;
return     Response  .  json  (  {     accepted  :     true     }  )  ;
}     catch     {
// Do not expose recipient addresses, message bodies or credentials.
return     new     Response  (  'Notification unresolved'  ,     {     status  :     500     }  )  ;
}
}
```


[Next.js Route Handlers](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/route) support


` request.text()


` for webhooks. Pass that unparsed body, the signature header and endpoint secret to Stripe’s documented


` constructEvent


` method. Calling


` request.json()


` first would break the raw-body verification contract.


The route retrieves the current invoice rather than treating an old event snapshot as current billing truth. Paid, void or otherwise non-open invoices are skipped. That check still has a race: payment can succeed after retrieval but before sending. The email therefore directs customers to the latest billing state instead of promising that the amount remains payable.


For a controlled sandbox send, set the sandbox Stripe customer’s email to an address you own or are authorized to contact. Do not use production customer records. Follow


[Stripe’s webhook setup](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks) to forward events to your local route:


```text
stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
```


Use the signing secret printed by that listener, not a different destination’s secret. Generate a failed invoice payment in your sandbox using Stripe’s documented test workflow. A generic payment-success trigger will be ignored because this route only handles


` invoice.payment_failed


` . Inspect the event delivery and provider response;


` accepted


` means provider acknowledgement, not delivery or an inbox open.


## How do you avoid duplicate notifications in production?


[Resend’s idempotency keys](https://resend.com/docs/dashboard/emails/idempotency-keys) suppress repeated requests with the same key and unchanged payload for 24 hours. This demo uses an event-specific key and refuses events at least 23 hours old. Age is only a conservative retry boundary—not proof that an event has been processed.


The limitation is deliberate: Stripe can retry beyond that window. A changed invoice amount, recipient or template can also make the same-key payload differ; Resend then rejects the conflict. Do not “fix” it by inventing a new key. Investigate the earlier request before deciding whether another email is appropriate.


Before enabling live events, replace the synchronous send with this application-owned sequence:


1. Verify the signature and atomically store a unique event record plus notification work in durable storage. Return success only after that work is safely recorded.


2. Let a worker retrieve billing state, check recipient/account policy, and freeze the exact message payload and template version before its first send attempt.


3. Record provider acknowledgement separately from delivery. Reuse the saved payload and key for eligible retries; reconcile ambiguous attempts after the provider’s window expires.


Persist skip reasons and operational failures too. Missing recipients, unsupported currencies and old events need an operator path, not repeated blind retries. Decide whether a separate failure attempt deserves a new reminder: an event-ID key does not limit the number of distinct failure events for one invoice. Stripe’s


[webhook guide](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks) also warns that events can arrive out of order.


## Where should the recovery button take customers?


Use your stable authenticated billing page. It should resolve the customer ID from the signed-in account, authorize access, then create a fresh


[Stripe Customer Portal session](https://docs.stripe.com/api/customer_portal/sessions/create) . Never accept an arbitrary customer ID from an email query string as authorization.


[Portal sessions are temporary](https://docs.stripe.com/billing/subscriptions/integrating-customer-portal) , so do not create one when preparing the message and expect it to work days later. The email opens your page; your page creates the session when needed. Make sure that page handles signed-out visitors and users who cannot manage billing without exposing someone else’s account.


Also review


[Stripe’s automatic customer-email settings](https://docs.stripe.com/billing/revenue-recovery/customer-emails) . Stripe can already send failed-payment notices. Choose which system owns this notification before turning on a custom workflow, rather than accidentally sending both.


## Frequently asked questions


### Does Elements process payments or send the email?


No. Elements renders content. Stripe owns billing events, Resend submits messages, and your application owns processing state, authorization and notification policy. Our


[transactional-email guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/transactional-emails) covers the broader message category.


### Can this handle every invoice currency or subscription state?


Not as written. The first version only sends for open, automatically collected USD invoices with a current customer email. It does not infer a subscription’s status from an invoice failure. Extend eligibility and formatting deliberately.


### Can customers edit this React template visually?


Not through this example. It is code-first. Elements can produce builder-compatible JSON for a separate visual editing workflow; visual changes do not reconstruct your React source. Our


[React builder guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/embeddable-react-email-builders-guide) explains that different integration path.


## Start with the rendering example, then connect your billing event


Explore the


[official runnable Next.js Elements example](https://github.com/unlayer/elements/tree/main/examples/nextjs-app-router) for email previews and an HTML endpoint—it demonstrates rendering, not a Stripe billing integration. Use the


[Elements rendering API](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/rendering-api) to implement your message, and


[start with Unlayer Elements](https://unlayer.com/elements) for the code-first content layer. Keep billing recovery and delivery responsibilities in the surrounding application.
